1)What is Coupling?

Coupling is the degree of dependency between two classes or modules.

If one class depends heavily on another, it is called Tight Coupling.
If one class depends very little on another, it is called Loose Coupling.

Definition (Exam):

Coupling is the measure of how strongly two classes or modules are connected or depend on each other.

Types of Coupling
1) Tight Coupling

In tight coupling, one class is directly dependent on another class.

Characteristics
High dependency.
Difficult to modify or test.
Reusability is low.
Example
class Engine {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Engine Started");
    }
}

class Car {
    Engine engine = new Engine();   // Direct dependency

    void drive() {
        engine.start();
        System.out.println("Car is moving");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car c = new Car();
        c.drive();
    }
}
Output
Engine Started
Car is moving
Explanation
Car directly creates an Engine object.
If the Engine class changes, the Car class may also need changes.
This is Tight Coupling.
2) Loose Coupling

In loose coupling, classes communicate through an interface or abstraction instead of depending directly on each other.

Characteristics
Low dependency.
Easy to maintain and test.
High reusability.
Flexible design.
Example
interface Engine {
    void start();
}

class PetrolEngine implements Engine {
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Petrol Engine Started");
    }
}

class DieselEngine implements Engine {
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Diesel Engine Started");
    }
}

class Car {
    Engine engine;

    Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }

    void drive() {
        engine.start();
        System.out.println("Car is moving");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Engine e = new PetrolEngine();
        Car c = new Car(e);
        c.drive();
    }
}
Output
Petrol Engine Started
Car is moving
Explanation
Car depends on the Engine interface, not a specific engine class.
You can easily replace PetrolEngine with DieselEngine without changing the Car class.
This is Loose Coupling.

2)What is Association in Java?

Association is an object-oriented relationship in which one object is connected to another object. Both objects are independent, meaning they can exist without each other.

Definition (Exam)

Association is a relationship between two independent classes where one object uses or is connected to another object.

Example (Teacher and Student)
A Teacher teaches a Student.
A Teacher can exist without a Student.
A Student can exist without a Teacher.

This is an Association relationship.

Java Example
class Teacher {
    String name;

    Teacher(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    void teach() {
        System.out.println(name + " is teaching.");
    }
}

class Student {
    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    void study(Teacher t) {
        System.out.println(name + " is studying from " + t.name);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Teacher t = new Teacher("Rahul");
        Student s = new Student("Amit");

        t.teach();
        s.study(t);
    }
}
Output
Rahul is teaching.
Amit is studying from Rahul

3)what is aggregation
What is Aggregation in Java?

Aggregation is a special type of Association that represents a weak "Has-A" relationship between two classes.

In Aggregation:

The child object can exist independently of the parent object.
If the parent object is destroyed, the child object still exists.
Definition (Exam)

Aggregation is a weak "Has-A" relationship where one class contains a reference to another class, and both objects can exist independently.

Real-Life Example
College and Student
A College has Students.
If the College is closed, the Students still exist and can join another college.

This is Aggregation.

Java Example
class Student {
    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

class College {
    String collegeName;
    Student student;

    College(String collegeName, Student student) {
        this.collegeName = collegeName;
        this.student = student;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println(student.name + " studies in " + collegeName);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student("Rahul");
        College c = new College("ABC College", s);

        c.display();
    }
}
Output
Rahul studies in ABC College
Explanation
College has a Student object.
The Student object is created outside the College class.
Even if the College object is deleted, the Student object still exists.
Therefore, it is Aggregation.
Characteristics of Aggregation
It represents a Has-A relationship.
It is a weak relationship.
Parent and child objects are independent.
Improves code reusability.


4)What is Composition in Java?

Composition is a special type of Association that represents a strong "Has-A" relationship between two classes.

In Composition:

The child object cannot exist without the parent object.
If the parent object is destroyed, the child object is also destroyed.
Definition (Exam)

Composition is a strong "Has-A" relationship in which one class contains another class, and the child object cannot exist independently of the parent object.

Real-Life Example
House and Room
A House has Rooms.
If the House is demolished, the Rooms also cease to exist.

This is Composition.

Java Example
class Room {
    Room() {
        System.out.println("Room Created");
    }
}

class House {
    private Room room;

    House() {
        room = new Room();   // Room is created inside House
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("House has a Room");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        House h = new House();
        h.display();
    }
}
Output
Room Created
House has a Room
Explanation
House creates the Room object inside its constructor.
The Room object belongs to the House.
If the House object is destroyed, the Room object is also destroyed.
Therefore, this is Composition.
Characteristics of Composition
It represents a strong Has-A relationship.
The child object depends on the parent object.
The child object cannot exist independently.
Used when one object completely owns another.

5)Difference Between Inheritance and Association in Java
Inheritance													Association
Represents an "Is-A" relationship.							Represents a "Uses-A" or "Has-A" relationship.
One class inherits properties and methods from another class.	One class is connected to or uses another class.
Uses the extends keyword.									Uses object references.
Child class can access parent class members.					Each class remains independent.
Used for code reusability.									Used to represent relationships between objects.
Example: Dog is an Animal.								Example: Teacher teaches Student

